  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">MELA for Her Re-useable Sanitary Pad Production in Ethiopia</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">MELA For Her (MFH) is a social enterprise that aims to transform women and girls&apos; lives across Ethiopia by providing them with access to affordable and eco -friendly menstrual products, and menstrual health education. MELA (??) in Amharic means SOLUTION. MFH&apos;s philosophy is based on the women to women model where women are the majority in the production, marketing and sales of its products as well as its main customers. The company is a socially and environmentally responsible business, bringing positive impact for local communities, particularly for women and girls.   The company generates its revenues from two main sources: production and sale of reusable sanitary pads/dignity kits (75 -80%) and provision of Menstrual Hygiene Management (MHM)/Menstrual Health and Hygiene (MHH) services (20 -25%).  The production of MFH&apos;s reusable sanitary pads is based on a hybrid production model that was awarded by The Good Business in March 2021. Under this model, the company produces sanitary pads in its own production unit (internal production), but when demand increases, it outsources part of the production to small and medium factories through contractual agreement (external production).   MFH produces two types of reusable sanitary pads (MELA Basic and MELA Premium). Its products have been tested by Ethiopian women and girls with excellent feedback and are available in the market. A prototype for a third product (MELA Eco), a product made from recycled and quality factory leftover fabrics, is ready for mass production. MFH also supplies basic and tailor -made dignity kits that include reusable sanitary pads, underwear, and soap, among other items, for humanitarian organizations. In addition, MFH provides MHM/MHH services such as training and research for Non Governmental Organisations (NGOs) and private companies.   During 2021, MFH reusable sanitary pads were sold exclusively by order. Customers were mainly NGOs working in emergency response and development programmes. In 202</narrative>
  </description>
